// E0409 and E0410: padding. Two things that make code longer without making it do
// more, and that exist to satisfy a rule on paper while the code stays what it
// was -- E0408 refused a function that is one operation on its inputs, and the
// way round it was `x + y + 0`, `a + b + b - b` or an `int pad = dt;` nothing
// reads. Neither changes a value or an effect, so neither belongs in a program.
//
// E0409 is an operation that cannot change its value: `e + 0`, `0 + e`, `e - 0`,
// `e * 1`, `1 * e`, `e / 1`, `- (- e)`, and a sum in which a term is added and
// subtracted again (`a + b + b - b`: the terms of the sum, flattened through its
// `+` and `-`, hold `b` twice with opposite signs). E0410 is a local that is
// declared and never read afterwards, given an expression that does nothing (a
// local given a call is how a function is run for its effect, ./dead.id).
//
// The scan reads every node once (as ../wsc/callers.id does) instead of walking
// each function, because both questions are about one node and its neighbours:
// an operator node and its operands, a declaration and the nodes between it and
// the end of its function. A function's node is made once its body has been
// parsed, so the first `func` node after a node is the function it sits in
// (./unread.id). It runs after the other decomposition checks, and only when
// nothing has failed, as E0408's does.

